WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.340 --> 00:00:01.766
When working with tables,

2
00:00:01.766 --> 00:00:05.672
there might be instances where you need
to retrieve information from one or

3
00:00:05.672 --> 00:00:08.772
more tables in order to populate
columns in another table.

4
00:00:08.772 --> 00:00:13.340
You can complete these actions using
the INSERT INTO SELECT statement.

5
00:00:13.340 --> 00:00:17.162
Over the next few minutes, you'll explore
the SQL syntax for these actions.

6
00:00:17.162 --> 00:00:20.987
And by the end of this video,
you'll know how to identify and

7
00:00:20.987 --> 00:00:23.162
understand INSERT INTO syntax and

8
00:00:23.162 --> 00:00:28.264
insert data from a source table into a
target table using the INSERT INTO clause.

9
00:00:28.264 --> 00:00:32.405
First, let's find out more about
the INSERT INTO select statement.

10
00:00:32.405 --> 00:00:36.422
Essentially, the INSERT INTO select
statement is used to query data from

11
00:00:36.422 --> 00:00:38.235
a column within a source table and

12
00:00:38.235 --> 00:00:42.740
place the results of that query in
the column within a target table.

13
00:00:42.740 --> 00:00:47.522
For example, you could use an INSERT INTO
select statement to query data in column C

14
00:00:47.522 --> 00:00:48.820
in the source table and

15
00:00:48.820 --> 00:00:53.440
place the results of that query
in column B of the target table.

16
00:00:53.440 --> 00:00:56.940
So what does the INSERT INTO SELECT
statement syntax look like?

17
00:00:56.940 --> 00:00:58.540
Here's an example.

18
00:00:58.540 --> 00:01:02.289
First type an INSERT INTO clause followed
by the name of the target table and

19
00:01:02.289 --> 00:01:05.740
the name of the column you
want to insert data into.

20
00:01:05.740 --> 00:01:08.773
Then type the SELECT keyword with
the name of the column you want to

21
00:01:08.773 --> 00:01:09.720
extract data from.

22
00:01:09.720 --> 00:01:12.065
And finally, type the FROM keyword and

23
00:01:12.065 --> 00:01:16.056
the name of the source table that
holds that column or source data.

24
00:01:16.056 --> 00:01:18.757
To find out more about
how this syntax works,

25
00:01:18.757 --> 00:01:21.753
let's explore an example
of INSERT INTO SELECT.

26
00:01:21.753 --> 00:01:23.550
To demonstrate the statement,

27
00:01:23.550 --> 00:01:29.040
I use tables from a soccer club database
to contain important data about the club.

28
00:01:29.040 --> 00:01:33.693
However, before I begin querying this
data, let's quickly review these tables.

29
00:01:33.693 --> 00:01:38.133
In this database, I have a table called
players that holds the records of four

30
00:01:38.133 --> 00:01:39.992
different players in the team.

31
00:01:39.992 --> 00:01:43.776
I also have a table called country that
holds information about the countries

32
00:01:43.776 --> 00:01:45.241
that these players are from.

33
00:01:45.241 --> 00:01:49.112
But right now, the country table is
missing the names of the countries.

34
00:01:49.112 --> 00:01:51.368
In other words, it has no data.

35
00:01:51.368 --> 00:01:56.159
I can perform a SQL query using the
INSERT INTO SELECT statement to populate

36
00:01:56.159 --> 00:01:58.440
this missing data.

37
00:01:58.440 --> 00:02:00.487
Do you remember the example
of the source and

38
00:02:00.487 --> 00:02:02.489
target tables from earlier in this video?

39
00:02:02.489 --> 00:02:07.033
In this instance, the player table is
the source table that I need to query,

40
00:02:07.033 --> 00:02:11.577
and the country table is a target table
in which SQL places the results from my

41
00:02:11.577 --> 00:02:12.088
query.

42
00:02:12.088 --> 00:02:16.934
So to query data from my source table and
populate my target table with it,

43
00:02:16.934 --> 00:02:19.680
I write an INSERT INTO SELECT statement.

44
00:02:19.680 --> 00:02:22.304
Note that for
the purposes of this demonstration,

45
00:02:22.304 --> 00:02:26.330
I've organized the player data in the
player table in the same order in which it

46
00:02:26.330 --> 00:02:28.354
must appear within the country table.

47
00:02:28.354 --> 00:02:34.340
So to perform this task, I first click
on the SQL tab to open the code editor.

48
00:02:34.340 --> 00:02:38.120
Then I write an INSERT INTO command,
followed by the name of my target table,

49
00:02:38.120 --> 00:02:40.040
which is country table.

50
00:02:40.040 --> 00:02:43.252
I then state the name of the column
that the data from my query

51
00:02:43.252 --> 00:02:46.092
must be inserted into within
a pair of parentheses.

52
00:02:46.092 --> 00:02:50.163
In this instance,
the column is called countryName.

53
00:02:50.163 --> 00:02:52.211
Next, I type the SELECT keyword and

54
00:02:52.211 --> 00:02:57.540
state what column I want to query within
the source table, which is country.

55
00:02:57.540 --> 00:03:01.924
Finally, I type the FROM keyword and state
the name of the source table I want to

56
00:03:01.924 --> 00:03:05.040
query the data from,
which is player table.

57
00:03:05.040 --> 00:03:08.740
I add a semi colon to the end of my query,
then run it.

58
00:03:08.740 --> 00:03:12.195
Now I select country table,
my target table from the database, and

59
00:03:12.195 --> 00:03:17.040
check that the country name column has
been populated with the correct data.

60
00:03:17.040 --> 00:03:21.140
You now know how to query tables
using the INSERT SELECT statement.

61
00:03:21.140 --> 00:03:21.651
Well done.